@*
    One group of rows in a task's detail pane, under the word that says what they
    have in common.

    The group is what the pane lays out, not the row. A pane that put its rows
    straight into columns would break "due date, reminder, repeat" across the gap
    and stand a due date next to a priority, which reads as a relationship
    neither of them has. Grouping first is what makes the columns mean something:
    a column is a subject, so the reader picks the subject and then the row
    instead of scanning every row for the one they wanted.

    The caption is a paragraph rather than a heading, and the group is named by
    it through aria-labelledby. A heading would have to guess its own level — a
    panel does not know how deep in a page it is, and a group that guessed wrong
    would put a hole in the document outline for the sake of two words. Naming
    the group from the caption rather than repeating it in aria-label keeps it one
    string: the same words twice is the same words twice to a screen reader.
*@

<div class="@ClassValue" role="group" aria-labelledby="@_captionId" data-testid="@TestId">
    <p class="task-action-group__caption" id="@_captionId">@Label</p>
    @ChildContent
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>What the rows in it have in common — "Scheduling", "Ranking".
    /// One word where one will do: this is a caption over three short rows, not
    /// a section heading.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Label { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The rows. <see cref="TaskAction"/> in every case so far, though
    /// nothing here requires it — a group is a labelled column, and what goes in
    /// a column is the pane's business.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    private readonly string _captionId = "task-action-group-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");

    private string ClassValue =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? "task-action-group" : $"task-action-group {CssClass}";
}
